 2. Why did you select this specific sub-theme and topic? Relate it to your experience / problem in your classroom/institution. (10 marks) 
(Give the background and rationale of the study)
Classroom Observation and Identified Problem
During my teaching practice as a B.Ed. trainee teacher, I observed that many Grade 5 students showed fear, frustration, and loss of confidence when they failed to answer science questions correctly or performed poorly in tests. Instead of viewing mistakes as part of learning, students often avoided participation after failure. This lack of resilience affected their engagement and achievement in science. Research shows that negative reactions to failure can reduce motivation and persistence in learning, especially at the elementary level, making it important to address this issue early (Martin & Marsh, 2014).
Importance of Resilience in Science Learning
Science learning naturally involves experimentation, trial, and error. Without resilience, students may give up quickly when results are not successful. Building resilience helps learners manage failure, reflect on mistakes, and try again with confidence. Studies emphasize that resilience is a key factor in academic success and emotional well-being, particularly in problem-solving subjects such as science. Developing resilience at the primary level can promote long-term positive learning attitudes and self-belief among students (Dweck, 2017).
Role of Activity Method in Addressing the Problem
The activity method encourages hands-on learning, collaboration, and exploration, allowing students to learn through doing rather than memorization. In my classroom, I noticed that students were more engaged and confident when learning through simple science activities. Activity-based learning reduces fear of failure by shifting focus from correct answers to the learning process. Research supports that active learning strategies help students cope with mistakes, improve motivation, and develop positive learning behaviors (Prince, 2014; Hmelo-Silver, 2016).
Gap Between Traditional Teaching and Student Needs
Traditional lecture-based teaching often limits student interaction and does not provide enough opportunities to learn from errors. This gap was evident in my classroom, where students hesitated to ask questions or attempt answers. Activity-based approaches create a supportive environment where failure is treated as a learning opportunity. Literature highlights that student-centered methods are more effective in meeting diverse learning needs and promoting emotional skills such as resilience (Fredricks, 2014; Jang, Kim, & Reeve, 2016).
Relevance of the Topic for Action Research
This topic was selected because it addresses a real classroom problem that directly affects students learning and confidence. As action research aims to improve teaching practice, studying the effect of the activity method on resilience is both practical and meaningful. It allows the teacher to reflect on instructional strategies and student responses. Recent studies stress that classroom-based interventions are effective in improving both academic and emotional outcomes, making this topic suitable for action research in science education (OECD, 2018; Yeager & Dweck, 2020).



