Review of Related Literature
Concept of Resilience in School Learning
Resilience in education refers to students ability to cope with challenges, setbacks, and failure while continuing to learn effectively. Asian researchers emphasize that resilience is not an inborn trait but a skill that can be developed through classroom practices. Wang and Gordon (2012) highlight that students in Asian contexts often face academic pressure, making resilience essential for sustaining motivation. Similarly, Chandra and Sharma (2018) argue that early development of resilience helps learners manage fear of failure and improves long-term academic outcomes, especially in demanding subjects like science.
Failure and Learning in Science Education
Failure is a natural part of science learning, as experimentation involves trial and error. However, many students perceive failure negatively due to exam-oriented systems common in Asian schools. According to Tan (2016), fear of making mistakes discourages students from active participation in science classrooms. Rizvi and Khan (2019) found that students who are not guided to reflect on failure often develop avoidance behaviors. These studies suggest that teaching approaches must reframe failure as a learning opportunity rather than a weakness.
Activity Method and Student Engagement
The activity method, also known as activity-based learning, focuses on hands-on tasks, experiments, and learner participation. Asian studies strongly support its effectiveness in primary education. Kaur (2014) reported that activity-based science teaching increased engagement and reduced anxiety among elementary students in India. Likewise, Lee and Kim (2020) found that students involved in structured activities showed higher confidence and persistence after unsuccessful attempts, indicating a positive link between active learning and resilience.
Activity-Based Learning and Emotional Development
Beyond academic achievement, activity-based learning contributes to students emotional and social growth. According to Ahmad and Iqbal (2021), Pakistani primary students exposed to collaborative activities developed better emotional control and peer support. Zhang (2017) also noted that interactive classroom activities promote a safe environment where students feel comfortable making mistakes. Such environments help students reflect on errors constructively, which is essential for building resilience from failure.
Research Gap and Relevance to the Present Study
Although existing Asian literature supports the role of activity-based learning in engagement and achievement, limited research specifically connects the activity method with resilience from failure in science at the primary level. Most studies focus on secondary or higher education. Rahman and Akhter (2023) stress the need for classroom-based action research to explore emotional outcomes of teaching methods. Therefore, the present study addresses this gap by examining how activity methods influence Grade 5 students resilience from failure in science within a Pakistani school context.
5. What was the major variables/construct of your project? Give definitions/ description from literature. (05 marks) 
(What are the key terms in your topic or study? what do you mean of these terms? What particular meaning you will attach to the term when used in this project?)
Independent Variable: Activity Method
The independent variable of this study is the activity method of teaching science. In this research, the activity method refers to the use of hands-on experiments, group work, demonstrations, and student-centered tasks during science lessons. The purpose of this method is to actively involve students in the learning process rather than relying on lecture-based instruction. In the context of this study, the activity method was implemented to help students explore scientific concepts through practice and reflection, allowing them to learn from mistakes in a supportive environment (Prince, 2014).

Dependent Variable: Resilience from Failure in Science
The dependent variable is resilience from failure in science. This refers to students ability to cope with mistakes, poor performance, or unsuccessful experiments without losing motivation. In this study, resilience was understood as students willingness to try again, remain engaged after failure, and show positive attitudes toward learning science. Improvement in resilience was observed through classroom behavior, participation, and responses to challenging tasks (Martin & Marsh, 2014).
Moderating Variable: Teacher Support and Feedback
The moderating variable in this research is teacher support and feedback. Teacher encouragement, positive feedback, and guidance can influence how effectively the activity method helps students build resilience. In this study, supportive teacher behavior was considered essential for creating a safe learning environment where failure was treated as part of learning. Research suggests that constructive feedback strengthens the impact of active learning on students emotional outcomes (Jang, Kim, & Reeve, 2016).
Control Variables: Grade Level, Curriculum, and Class Size
The control variables include grade level, curriculum content, and class size. The study was limited to Grade 5 students, followed the same science curriculum, and was conducted in one class to ensure consistency. These variables were controlled to reduce their influence on the dependent variable, allowing clearer observation of the effect of the activity method on resilience (Creswell, 2014).




