Participant Profile
The participants of this action research study were students from Grade 5 at School name _______________, _____________. A total of 30 students took part in the research, selected from a class of 45 students. All participants were boys, as the school is a boys institution. The age of the students ranged from 10 to 11 years, which is typical for Grade 5 at the primary level. The students were studying science as a compulsory subject under the provincial curriculum. Most of the participants had prior exposure to traditional teaching methods and limited experience with activity-based learning, making them suitable for examining changes in resilience and learning behavior.
Selection Criteria
The participants were selected using purposive sampling, as the study aimed to focus on students who were directly experiencing the identified classroom problem. Students from one intact Grade 5 section were chosen to ensure consistency in teaching conditions. From this section, 30 students were included based on regular attendance, willingness to participate, and parental consent where required. Students with irregular attendance were excluded to maintain continuity during the intervention period. The selection was also influenced by the feasibility of managing activities and observations within a single classroom setting, which is appropriate for action research conducted by a B.Ed. trainee teacher.
General Characteristics
The selected students came from mixed socio-economic backgrounds, with most belonging to middle-income families. Their parents were engaged in occupations such as government service, small businesses, private jobs, and daily wage labor. The majority of students lived in nuclear family systems, while some belonged to joint families. Academically, students showed varying levels of achievement in science, ranging from average to below average, with some high achievers as well. Many students initially displayed low confidence after failure in science tasks but showed curiosity and interest during hands-on activities. Overall, the group represented a typical urban public-school classroom, making the findings relevant for similar educational settings.
